`lib/Net/Async/Zitadel/OIDC.pm:119-150` stores the pending JWKS Future in `_jwks_inflight` and clears it on success (line 140) and in `on_fail` (line 143). Cancellation triggers neither. If the only consumer cancels - a caller wrapping `verify_token_f` in `Future->wait_any` with a timeout, or dropping a request - the cancel propagates up into the stored Future, and from then on line 119 returns that cancelled Future to everyone.

Reproduce with a mock `http` whose `GET` returns a pending Future:

    my $f1 = $oidc->verify_token_f('x.y.z'); $f1->cancel;
    $oidc->_jwks_inflight->is_cancelled   # 1
    $oidc->jwks_f->is_cancelled           # 1 - and for every further call

`verify_token_f` only recovers by accident: `then` on a cancelled Future (Future 0.52) runs the callback with no arguments, `_decode_jwt(kid_keys => undef)` dies, and the retry path forces a refresh. With `no_retry => 1`, and for direct `jwks_f` callers, the object stays broken until something forces a refresh.

Related in the same function: forced refreshes are never coalesced (`$force` skips both the read at line 119 and the store at line 150), so N concurrent rejected tokens start N JWKS fetches.

Done: clear the slot with `on_cancel` too (or store a `without_cancel` copy and hand each caller its own derived Future); coalesce forced refreshes as well; a test cancels the first caller and asserts the second gets a live Future and exactly one new request.

